  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday through Wednesday: An upper level low pressure system \nis set to strengthen just off the northern British Columbia \ncoast, brushing the northwest portion of the state of Washington\nas it does. This low will be too far northward to result in \nprecipitation, but it will bring colder temperatures and \nelevated winds. While highs on Monday were in the mid 80s, highs\ntoday will drop down into the high 70s, and will drop further \ninto the low 70s by Wednesday. A west-east pressure gradient \nwill strengthen across the state, bringing elevated winds to the\nforecast area, particularly along the Cascade gaps, which look \nto see wind gusts 15 to 20 mph and localized wind gusts up to 20\nto 25 mph.\n\nThursday through Sunday: Another trough will drop down due to a\ndisturbance off the Gulf of Alaska, strengthening off the PNW \ncoast. As it does, higher heights will briefly brush the area as\na broad ridge sets up in the central US. Temperatures will \nbriefly rise back into the high 70s, with some isolated 80 \ndegree areas. This will be short lived, as the trough will then \nmove inland with the center of the low positioned over \nWashington. This will usher in a strong cold front Thursday \nnight into Friday, with widespread precipitation chances for the\nInland Northwest. Models have come into better agreement on the\nchances for precipitation, though now the GFS is the drier \nmodel as it shows a weaker low moving inland than the ECMWF, \nstill creating some uncertainty in precipitation amounts.\n\nPrecipitation will start in the mountain areas on Thursday \nevening, and then move across the forecast area through Friday \nmorning. It looks to mostly move out of the area by Saturday \nnight, though the ECMWF has a slower moving solution that shows \nprecipitation lingering in the northern mountains and ID \nPanhandle through Sunday. The GFS has the system moving more \nquickly out of the area. NBM probabilities show a 30 percent \nchance or greater of at least 0.10 inches of rain across the \nentire forecast area through Saturday morning. Areas that will \nsee the highest rainfall include the Cascades, northern \nmountains, Idaho Panhandle, which look to receive a quarter inch\nor more of rain and isolated rain totals up to 0.40 inches. \nAdditionally, snow levels look to drop low enough to allow the \nhighest elevations of the Cascades (6000-7000 feet and above) to\nsee a couple inches of snow. Elevations 5000 feet and above \nwill see under an inch of snow. We will keep a close eye on snow\nlevels through the week, as should any travelers or hikers \nanticipating being in the mountains this weekend. \n\nForcing associated with the incoming low combined with a couple\nhundred joules of SBCAPE will support chances for thunderstorms\nFriday afternoon and evening. Much of the forecast area aside \nfrom the deeper Columbia basin sees a 10 to 15 percent chance of\nthunderstorms. PWATs higher than normal at 0.8 inches and above\nand saturated mid and low level relative humidity values (90% \nand above) point to isolated heavy rainfall rates being the main\nthunderstorm threats. \n\nWinds will increase with the cold frontal passage Friday \nafternoon and evening, and have progressively gotten stronger \nwith each model run. The areas that will see the highest wind \ngusts will be the Waterville Plateau and Columbia Basin areas, \nwhere wind gusts 30-35 mph are possible. Elsewhere, particularly\nacross the Wenatchee, Spokane, and Pullman areas, wind gusts \n25-30 mph are expected. These wind gusts are 10-15 mph higher \nthan what models had yesterday, and will be closely monitored \nfor any further increases. /AS",
